  <Table Name="Quote" Columns="ID:System.Int64,Quote:System.String,Source:System.String" Num="342">
    <Row>1,"Roll on, thou deep and dark blue ocean - roll! Man marks the earth with ruin - his control stops with the shore",Lord Byron</Row>
    <Row>2,It is useful to test prospective management strategies against ecosystem models: if they don't work on simple models why should they work in reality,"Keith Sainsbury (ICES/SCOR Conference, Montpellier March 1999)"</Row>
    <Row>3,Sustainable management of fisheries cannot be achieved without an acceptance that the goals of fisheries management are they same as those of environmental conservation,"Katherine Richardson (ICES/SCOR Conference, Montpellier, March 1999)"</Row>
    <Row>4,"Predators are often considered objects of displeasure, but to see a pike stalk and capture its prey is to witness all the skills, which have made bony fish as agile in their aquatic environment as birds are in the air",Tony Pitcher and Paul Hart 1982</Row>
    <Row>5,"The interactions between the ecological drama at levels ranging from individuals to ecosystems, and the physical dimensions of the stage on which it is played out, are important in understanding how the world works",R.M. May 1989</Row>
    <Row>6,Ecopath and its associated analyses represent a macroscope ('ecoscope') through which to view the structure and functioning of entire ecosystems,R.E. Ulanowicz 1993</Row>
    <Row>7,Modeling is much too important to be left to modelers,F.E.A. Wood 1978 (Walters 1986)</Row>
    <Row>8,"The construction of quantitative models is an important step in problem analysis, but the models should not be seen as final products",C.J. Walters 1986</Row>
    <Row>9,The existence of large uncertainty about system response need not imply that the best action is also uncertain,C.J. Walters 1986</Row>
    <Row>10,"3rd Fisherman: Master, I marvel how the fishes live in the sea. 1st Fisherman: Why, as men do a-land -- the great ones eat up the little ones","Shakespeare, Pericles, II:1"</Row>
    <Row>11,"To deal with natural sciences is of no use at all. One is defenseless and out of control. The scientist immediately begins to distract with this details. Now he is off to Australia, now to the moon, now down into a cavern in the ground, now the hell up the ass - after an intestinal worm; now the telescope is used, now the microscope: Who can stand it, damn it all!",Soren Kirkegaard (Translated by Hans Schroeder)</Row>
    <Row>12,The principles of thermodynamics occupy a special place among the laws of nature … there is no natural process to which they cannot be applied,W. Ernst</Row>
    <Row>13,"Biological evolution involves a hierarchical ascendency to more and more complex forms of living systems. The second law of thermodynamics asserts that the universe, or any isolated section of the universe that we choose to consider, is tending toward maximum entropy.",H.J. Morowitz 1968</Row>
    <Row>14,The unfathomable complexity of reality drives man to seek practical accomodation with it. The model serves well in this role. The mathematical form of a model is unique in permitting mechanization and automation of man's intellect - indeed in amplifying this capacity,J. Richardson 1984</Row>
    <Row>15,Canst thou draw out leviathan with an hook? Or his tongue with a cord which thou lettest down? Canst thou put a hook into his nose? Or bore his jaw through with a thorn? Shall the companions make a banquet of him? Shall they part him among the merchants? Canst thou fill his skin with barbed irons? Or his head with fish spears?,"Job 41: 1, 2, 6, 7"</Row>
    <Row>16,Most of the bays and harbours abound with right whale … and the inhabitants of Hobart Town have frequently witnessed the whole method of killing them from their windows,Godwin's Emigrant Guide to Van Diemen's Land (1823)  (in Colwell 1995)</Row>
    <Row>17,"The [whales] are exceedingly plentiful on our coast [by Derwent River, Tasmania] this season, and have not been known to be as numerous at any former period.","The Colonist newspaper, 24 August 1832, Tasmania (in Colwell 1995)"</Row>
    <Row>18,"Thus, almost at our very door or threshold, are we provided with the means of becoming rich, with little comparative trouble or exertion. [About bay whaling]","Henry Melville, Van Diemen's Land, 1833  (in Colwell 1995)"</Row>
    <Row>19,"Within 3 leagus of the shore [near Port Jackson, New South Wales] we saw sperm whales in great plenty. We sailed through different shoals of them from 12 o'clock in the day till sunset, all around the horizon, as far as I could see from the mast head","Captain Thomas Melville, Captain of Britania, letter of 22 November 1791  (in Colwell 1995)"</Row>
    <Row>20,Models are not like religion -- you can have more than one,V.C.</Row>
    <Row>21,"The oceans, unlike forests, still look like the oceans after we've removed their contents, and even scientists are susceptible to being seduced to ignore phenomena that are out of sight",Carlton 1998</Row>
    <Row>22,"In 1956, the Icelandic Government paid the United States Navy almost £100,000 to bombard schools of killer whales with depth charges, to save their southern coast fishing grounds",Colwell 1995</Row>
    <Row>23,"Between 1820 and 1850, the heyday of sperm whaling, an estimated 70,000 men from America, England, France and Australia killed appromimately 10,000 sperm whales a year",Colwell 1995</Row>
    <Row>24,"In the early 1800s, whalers putting out from American and British ports could count on filling up within two years. By 1844 it took anything up to 4 years.",Colwell 1995</Row>
    <Row>25,"South for whales. Hopes for kill worth £4,000,000. What is expected to prove the greatest slaughter of whales the world has ever known has just begun. It is estimated that 25,000 whales will have been killed when the season ends in March. A fleet of 129 vessels, of which 17 are mother ships, …, has gone from Norway to South Georgia","Adelaide Advertiser, 1 October 1932 (in Colwell 1995)"</Row>
    <Row>26,"During the twenty blank years of deep sea whaling which began in 1875, when whalers all over the world were forced into a retirement from which many of them would never return, the whale population of the world was left to increase. The sperm, and to a lesser extent the right whale, evidently made the most of this opportunity",Colwell 1995</Row>
    <Row>27,"In 1897, over 2000 whales were taken off Newfoundland alone",Colwell 1995</Row>
    <Row>28,"Captain Larsen … in 1922 … with a small fleet of a 12,000 ton freighter and five small whale chasers took 200 blue whales (in the Ross Sea)",Colwell 1995</Row>
    <Row>29,"A harpoon gunner named Jorgensen, working on a chaser belonging to the Sir James Clark Ross, killed 245 whales in the 1930-31 season in the Ross Sea",Colwell 1995</Row>
    <Row>30,"The Germans, the English, and the Japanese followed the Norwegians into southern waters and in the 1934-35 season some 40,000 whales were killed and processed",Colwell 1995</Row>
    <Row>31,"The 12th day of April, having then been 27 days from Poole, we came on soundings and knew it to be the Bank of Newfoundland. We should have understood this well enough form other indications for it seemed as if all the Fowles of Air were gathered thereunto. They so bemused the eye with their perpetual comings and goings that their numbers quite defied description. There can be but few places on Earth where is to be seen such a manifestation of the fecundity of His Creation","Eighteenth Century commentary, See Mowat, 1984: 18."</Row>
    <Row>32,"This island (Isle of Birds, NE Newfoundland) is so exceedingly full of birds (great auks) that all the ships of France might load a cargo of them without anyone noticing that any had been removed.",Jacques Cartier in 1535. See Mowat 1984 p. 23</Row>
    <Row>33,"We came to . . . two islands (in Gulf of St. Laurence) . . . as completely covered with birds (Great Auks), which nests there, as a field is covered with grass . . . we landed on the lower part of the smaller island and killed more than a thousand (Great auks).",Jacques Cartier in 1534. See Mowat 1984 p. 25</Row>
    <Row>34,This path has been laid before you. The choice is yours.,Star Wars I</Row>
    <Row>35,"By A.D. 900, the spearbill (great auk) was no longer being killed primarily for food; it was being slaughtered for its oil and for its soft, elastic feathers, ...  By the mid-1500s, only a scattering of its eastern Atlantic breeding colonies had escaped destruction. By a century later only one remained, on the bleak and forbidding island of St. Kilda in the Outer Hebrides... Some time before 1800 it went away from St.Kilda for the last time . . . never to return",Mowat 1984</Row>
    <Row>36,"In the air and round about on the water are a hundred as many as on the island (Isle of Birds, NE Newfoundland). Some of these birds (great auks) are as large as geese, being black and white with a beak like a raven. These stay always in the water, not being able to fly in the air because they have only small wings, ... In less than half-an-hour our two barcques were laden with them as if laden with stones.",Jacques Cartier in 1534. See Mowat 1984 p. 23</Row>
    <Row>37,"The finding of spearbill (great auk) bones in Indian middens along the New England coast, and even as far south as Florida, establishes the fact that they were once found far to the south of the range currently ascribed to them by many biologists","Mowat 1984, p 28"</Row>
    <Row>38,"In the 1570s, Captain Anthony Parkhurst wrote: at an Island named Pengwin we may drive them (great auks) on a plank into our ship, as many as will lade her . . . There is more meat on one of them than on a goose. The Frenchmen that fish near the Grand Bay do bring small store of meat withthem, but victual themselves always with these birds","Mowat 1984, p 29"</Row>
    <Row>39,"Edwartd Hayes, master of one of Sir Humphreys Gilbert's ships, in the late 16th century described an island named Penguin [because] of a fowle [great auk] there breeding in incredible abundance, which cannot fly . . . which the Frenchman take without difficulty . . . to barrel up with salt","Mowat 1984, p 29"</Row>
    <Row>40,"Around 1600, Richard Whitbourne noted: These Penguins [great auks] are as big as geese and . . . they multiply so infinetely upon a certain flat island that men drive them from hence upon a board, into their boats by the hundreds at a time, as if God had made the innocency of so poor a creature to become such an admirable instrument for the sustenation of man.","Whitbourne 1620, in Mowat 1984"</Row>
    <Row>41,Funk Island (Newfoundland is a baren spot inhabited only be Penguins (great auk) and other Birds. The astonishing quantity which resort to this Island is beyond ... Belief. As soon as you put your foot on shore you meet with such Thousands of the birds that you cannot find a place for your feet and they are so lazy that they will not attempt to move of your way.,"Thomas, Aaron. In Mowat 1984, p 35"</Row>
    <Row>42,"You may know you are on the Bank (Grand Banks) by the great quantities of fowles but none are to be minded so much as the Pengwin (great auk), for these never go without the bank as the others do, for they are always on it","Rutters and Pilots, The English Pilot, 1774 Edition. See Mowat 1984, p. 36."</Row>
    <Row>43,"This sure sea-mark (great auk's) on the Grand Banks has now totally disappeared, from the ruthless trade in eggs and skins.","Sir Richard Bonnycastle, 1792. Mowat, 1984, p. 36"</Row>
    <Row>44,"The Geirfugl's (great auk's) disappearance must not be regarded as a migration, much less a natural dying out, but as an extirpation [that] has its chief cause in the devastations wrought by men","Prof. J. Steenstrup, 1855. See Mowat 1984, p. 37"</Row>
    <Row>45,"No matter how many there may have been, the Great Auk had to go. They must have consumed thousands of tons of marine life that commercial fish stocks depend on. There wasn't room for them in any properly managed fishery. Personally, I think we ought to be grateful to the old timers for handling that problem for us.","Anon. functionary of the Department of Fisheries and Oceans, Canada. See Mowat, 1984, p. 39."</Row>
    <Row>46,"The sea fowles do not only feed those who trade [to Newfoundland] but also they are a great furthering of divers ships voyages, because the abundance of them is such that the fishermen do bait their hooks with the quarters of Seafowle on them: and therewith some ships do yearly take a great part of their fishing voyages, with such bait.","Late 1500s, Whitbourne 1620, see Mowat, 1984, p. 41."</Row>
    <Row>47,"So great an abundance of all kinds [of seabirds] that all my crew and myself, having cut clubs for ourselves, killed so great a number [at the rookeries at Sambro Island near Halifax] ... that we were unable to carry them away. And aside from these the number of those which were spared and which rose into the air, made a cloud so thick that the rays of the sun could scarcely penetrate it.","Denys, Nicolas. 1672. See Mowat, 1984, p . 41"</Row>
    <Row>48,"In 1580, more than 300 European ships were already fishing the northeastern approaches [to North America], and that number quadrupled before 1700. In 1784, there were 540 deep-sea vessels alone, most of them using birds for bait during at least part of the fishing season","Mowat 1984, p 41."</Row>
    <Row>49,"Even cormorants were used for bait. Althought initially their colonies [in North America] were to be found everywhere along the coasts at least as far south as Georgia, by 1922 they had been so reduced that the great cormorant was for a time thought to have been extirpated as a breeding bird in North America","Mowat 1984, p . 42"</Row>
    <Row>50,"At length we discovered at a distance a white speck which the pilot assured us was the celebrated rock [Bird Rocks, Gulf of St. Lawrence] We thought it was still covered with snow several feet deep. As we approached I imagined that the atmoshpere around us was filled with [snow] flakes but . . I was assured there was nothing in sight but the gannets and their island home. I rubbed my eyes, took out my glasses, and saw the strange dimness in the air was caused by the innumerable birds","Audobon, J.J. 1833, see Mowat 1984, p. 44"</Row>
    <Row>51,"Since the establishment of a drift-net fishery for Atlantic Salmon off the west coast of Greenland in the late 1960s, an average of a 250,000 murres have been drowned annually, and, in one year alone, 500,000 to 750,000 of them perished in the nets","Mowat 1984, p. 49"</Row>
    <Row>52,"On the island of Røst off the northwest coast of Norway some 500,000 seabird chicks were being hatched every spring. In 1977 only one chick in 1,000 survived, the rest dying mainly because of starvation -- attributed to [fishery induced] depletion of herring stocks.","Mowat 1984, p 50"</Row>
    <Row>53,"All my people are so surfeited with game . . . they wish no more, whether Wild Geese, Ducks, Teal, Plover, Snipe ... [even] our dogs lie beside this meat [without touching it] so much are they satiated with it ... So great an abundance of Wild Geese, Ducks and Brant is seen that is not believable, and they all make so great a noise at night that one has trouble to sleep.","Denys 1672, see Mowat 1984, p 75"</Row>
    <Row>54,"In times past, the white [polar] bear was not restricted to polar regions either in Europe or in Asia. Nor was it in America. In fact, the animal most often singled out for notice by early voyagers to the northeastern coasts of the New World was the white bear. As the historic record clearly shows, this was not due to its novelty but to the fact that it was one of the commonest large mammals to be seen.","Mowat 1984, p. 95"</Row>
    <Row>55,"The earliest account of encounters with white bears is in Jacques Cartier's voyage of 1534. A white bear was found on Funk Island [Newfoundland], where it had probably been living the life of Riley on a diet of great auks.","Mowat 1984, p. 95"</Row>
    <Row>56,"At least twenty White Bear Bays, Lakes, Rivers, Coves and Islands still dot the map of Newfoundland. Together with a good many more on Labrador, they testify to the one-time presence and abundance of their majestic namesake throughout this region.","Mowat 1984, p. 96"</Row>
    <Row>57,"In the 16th Century Basque whalers began to work the northern coast of the Gulf [of St Lawrence]. Thereafter, the number of whale carcasses increased at such a rate that not all the [white] bears in eastern North America could have consumed this bounty. Such a glut of food became available as a result of the Basque slaughter of the great whales that the white bear population seems to have exploded. So numerous did they become that the entire region began to acquire a singularly menacing reputation.","Mowat 1984, p. 98"</Row>
    <Row>58,In late April of 1776 one of [Captain George Cartwrights] men saw the tracks of near a hundred white-bears which had lately crossed Sandwich Bay (SE Labrador),"Cartwright, in Mowat 1984"</Row>
    <Row>59,"Ice seals were found in near astronomical numbers off Newfoundland, south Labrador, and in the Gulf of St. Lawrence from December through to April. Furthermore, white bears will happily eat any species of seal and, in aboriginal times, both grey and harbour seals existed in enormous quantities not only in all the waters just mentioned, but south to Cape Cod and beyond.","Mowat 1984, p. 103"</Row>
    <Row>60,It has been the custom of Arctic travellers to kill all the Polar Bears they could… One Arctic explorer told me that he personally had killed 200 Polar Bears and had secured but few of them.,"Ernest Thompson Seton, 1909"</Row>
    <Row>61,[The Grand Banks are so] swarming with fish [that they] could be taken not only in with a net but in baskets let down [and weighted] with a stone.,"John Cabot, 1497, see Mowat 1984, p. 166"</Row>
    <Row>62,"[The lower St Lawrence] river ... is the richest in every kind of fish that anyone remembers ever having seen or heard of; for from its mouth to its head you will find in their season the majority of the varieties of salt- and fresh-water fish ... great numbers of mackerel, mullet, sea bass, tunnies, large eels ... Quantities of lampreys and salmon ... [in the upper River] are many pike, trout, carp, bream and other fresh-water fish","Jacques Cartier 1535, see Mowat 1984, p. 166"</Row>
    <Row>63,"A little Boye might take of Cunners and Pinacks and such delicate fish at the ships sterne, more than six or ten men can eat in a daie; but with a casting Net, [he could take] thousands .. Cod, Cuske, Halibut, Mackerell, Scate or such like, a man may take with a hooke and line what he will ... no River where there is not plenty of Sturgeon or Salmon or both; all of which are to be had in abundance","Captain John Smith describing New England fisheries, 1614. See Mowat 1984, p 166"</Row>
    <Row>64,"Here [Gulf of St Lawrence about 1680] also are seen prodigious quantities of all kinds of fish, Cod,  Salmon, Herring, Trout, Bass, Mackerel, Flounder, Shad, Sturgeon, Pickerel, Oysters, Smelt, Skate, Whitefish …","Mowat 1984, p. 166"</Row>
    <Row>65,"In the sea adjacent [to Newfoundland, Cabot] found so great a quantity ... of great fish ... called baccalaos that at times they even stayed the passage of his ships.","Peter Martyr, ca 1516. See Mowat 1984, p. 167"</Row>
    <Row>66,"When the Grace of Bristol sheltered at the island of St. Pierre [SE of Newfoundland] in 1594, her people laid the ship upon the lee, and in 2 houres space we tooke with our hooks 3 or 4 hundred great Cod for the provision of our ship","Mowat 1984, p. 168"</Row>
    <Row>67,About this Island there is as great an abundance of cods as is any place to be found. In a little more than an houre we caught with hookes 250 of them,"Charles Leigh, Magdalen Islands, Gulf of St. Lawrence, 1597. See Mowat 1984, p. 168"</Row>
    <Row>68,"Cods are so thick by the shore that we hardly have been able to row a boat through them ... Three men going to Sea in a boat, with some on shore to dress and dry them, in 30 days will commonly kill between 25 and 30 thousand.","John Mason, English fishing skipper working out of a Newfoundland shore station, ca 1600. See Mowat 1984, p. 168."</Row>
    <Row>69,"In the late 1500s fishing skipper Richard Whitbourne wrote that the average lading for any given ship tallied 125,000 cod. These were from virgin cod populations producing fish up to six or seven feet in length and weighing as much as 200 pounds. The annual cod fishery in the northeastern approaches then yielded about 368,000 tons.","Mowat 1984, p. 168"</Row>
    <Row>70,"In 1747, 564 French vessels manned by 27,500 fishers brought home codfish worth a million pounds sterling [from the Great Banks]","Mowat 1984, p. 169."</Row>
    <Row>71,"[In 1876 John Rowan went aboard] a schooner cod-fishing close to shore .. They were fishing in about three fathoms of water and we could see the bottom actually paved with codfish. I caught a dozen in about fifteen minutes; my next neighbour [a crewman] on the deck of the schooner, caught three times as many, grumbling all the time that it was the worst fishing season he had ever known",Rowan 1876</Row>
    <Row>72,"Between 1899 and 1904, the annual catch of cod (and of haddock, which in the salt-fish business was treated as cod) for Grand Banks/Newfoundland waters approached a million tons... In 1956, cod landings were down to 80,000 metric tons","Mowat 1984, p. 170"</Row>
    <Row>73,"One morning we raised forty or fifty paired Spanish draggers Working Green Bank (Grand Banks area) .. Some of them seemed to have a tail.. When we came over them at about 2,000 feet, we saw it was dead fish. There must have been millions of them stretching out astern of each boat that had just hauled its net and was sorting the catch on deck. Undersized fish [haddock] were going overside like confetti","Mowat 1984, p. 172, citing a pilot in the Royal Canadian Air Force, who flew patrols over Canada's coastal waters in the 1950s."</Row>
    <Row>74,"The late Monsieur de la Tour has a weir built in which were caught great numbers of these Gaspereaux which were salted down for winter. Sometimes they were caught in so great a quantity that he was obliged to break the weir and throw them into the sea, as otherwise they would have befouled the weir which would thus have been ruined","Citation in Mowat 1984, p. 176"</Row>
    <Row>75,It is an astonishing sight to paddle down the Restigouche and see the farmers smelting -- scooping up the little fish in handnets. The amount they take is incredible and most of their potatoes spring from this fishy manure,"Citation in Mowat 1984, p. 176"</Row>
    <Row>76,Herring abound in countless shoals. Anyone not familiar with northern waters will suspect me of romancing when I say that I have seen 600 barrels taken in one sweep of a seine net. Often sufficient salt cannot be procured to save them and they are used as manure,"Citation in Mowat 1984, p. 176"</Row>
    <Row>77,"An American schooner struck a school of mackerel ... And before midnight, fishing with hook and line, the crew had 100 barrels caught ... Fish are destroyed and wasted in the most reckless manner, but the supply never fails. For a week in the spring, smelts run up all the rivers in an unceasing stream","Citation in Mowat 1984, p. 176"</Row>
    <Row>78,Men scarce past middle life tell of seeing three hundred herring vessels off their shore at one time ... Of seeing Pleasant Bay so packed with herrings that men had only to dip them up until their vessel was full.,"Citation in Mowat 1984, p. 176"</Row>
    <Row>79,"When the capelin drove up on the beaches of Conception Bay to spawn we would stant up to our knees in a regular soup of them, scooping them out with buckets and filling the wagons until the horses could hardly haul them off the beaches. You would sink to your ankles in the sand, it was that spongy with capelin eggs.","Citation in Mowat 1984, p. 176"</Row>
    <Row>80,The run of squid was so heavy that the boats were filled with scoop nets instead of chatching them with jiggers. At low tide so many got left behind that the foreshore was coated a food deep and out a hundred paces from high water mark.,"Citation in Mowat 1984, p. 176"</Row>
    <Row>81,"During the latter part of the nineteenth century and into the twentieth, more than 400 salmon clubs fished the rivers of the northeastern seaboard from Maine to Labrador","Mowat 1984, p. 180"</Row>
    <Row>82,The earliest North American reference to Atlantic salmon is contained in the saga account of Leif Erikson's voyage Vinland in 995 when he overwintered somewhere on the coast of Newfoundland. There was no shortage of salmon there and these were larger salmon than they had ever seen before.,"Mowat 1984, p. 180"</Row>
    <Row>83,"By conservative estimate, at least 3,000 salmon rivers [at the time the European invasion of he Americas began] provided several hundreds of thousands of spawning beds for an overall Atlantic population that may well have outnumbered that of the several salmon species found on the Pacific coast of North America","Mowat, 1984, p. 181"</Row>
    <Row>84,"So large a quantity of Salmon enters [Miramichi River that] at night one is unable to sleep, so great is the noise they make in falling upon the water after having thrown or darted themselves into the air [while] passing over the river flats","Denys 1672, writing of the early years of the 17th Century, see Mowat 1984, p 181"</Row>
    <Row>85,"[Near Chedabucto Bay on Cape Breton Island] I found a little river which I have named Rivère du Saulmon ... I made a cast of the seine net at its entrance where it took so great a quantity of Salmon that ten men could not haul it to land and ... had it not broken, the Salmon would have carried it off. We had a boat full of them, the smallest three feet long ... [On Baie Chaleur] there is found a little river with Salmon of extraordinary length, some have been taken of six feet in length.","Denys 1672, writing of the early years of the 17th Century, see Mowat 1984, p 181"</Row>
    <Row>86,"John Smith reported that, in what would become New England, there was no river where there is not plenty of salmon, a statement echoed by all the early English journalists. The French were equally impressed. Pierre Boucher, in about 1650, marveled at the many fine rivers abounding with fish, especially salmon; there are prodigious quantities of that fish there.","Mowat 1984, p. 181"</Row>
    <Row>87,This Fish [sturgeon] is here in great plenty and in some Rivers so numerous that it is hazardous for Canoes and the like light Vessels to pass to and fro.,"Josselyn 1670s, see Mowat 1984, p. 181"</Row>
    <Row>88,"The Sieur de Courtemanche, describing a journey along the north shore of the Gulf of St. Laurence sometime around 1705, makes a litany out of the salmon rivers: Muskware Bay, into which flow two rivers very rich in salmon .. Washikuti Bay, equally rich in salmon .. Etamamu River, full of salmon .. Eskimo River, rich in salmon of extraordinary size .. Blanc Sablon River, plenty of very good salmon.","Mowat 1984, p. 182"</Row>
    <Row>89,"George Cartwright fished salmon for export in the 1770s from several southeast Labrador rivers. The run in White Bear River, he noted, was so thick that a ball could not be have been fired in the river without striking a salmon. He and a crew of three men killed 12,396 in one season on one river, and he lamented that he could and would have easily killed 30,000 if only he had not run out of salt with which to cure them","Cartwright, G. 1792, see Mowat 1984, p. 182"</Row>
    <Row>90,"By the end of the eighteenth century, Newfoundland alone was exporting 5,000 tierce [of salmon, corresponding to 2,500,000 pounds] a year and it is estimated that the North American fishery was exporting annually in excess of 30 million pounds, live weight","Mowat 1984, p. 182"</Row>
    <Row>91,"Previous to the separation from Maine, complained a Boston man in the 1820s, large quantities of salmon were packed in Massachusetts; since 1818 none have been ... The building of dams and manufacturing establishments ... has almost annihilated them in this commonwealth.","Mowat 1984, p. 183"</Row>
    <Row>92,"The total amount [of salmon] exported [from Baie Chaleur, New Brunswick] in 1870 must be very large . . . One American firm puts up as much as 280,000 lbs here in a season. . . Sometimes whole boat loads will average 25 lbs each fish, and I have seen fish here up to 56 lbs in weight.","Citation in Mowat 1984, p. 183"</Row>
    <Row>93,"In 1890 the kill [of sturgeon] in the Delaware River alone amounted to more than 5 million pounds. As late as in 1897, one group of fishermen was able to kill 335 large, spawning-run sturgeon in a single haul at Amaganset.","Mowat 1984, p. 190"</Row>
    <Row>94,"On September 6, 1535, the vessels of Jacques Cartier's second expedition anchored under the lee of the Isle au Coudres at Baie St-Paul in the St. Lawrence River. After noting the presence there of schools of white whales, Cartier's scribe added: In this bay, and about this island there are inestimable numbers 'de grande tortures'  -- of large turtles.","Mowat 1984, p. 188"</Row>
    <Row>95,"Josselyn, in New England ca 1670, not only listed five sorts of Sea-Turtles, but watched from shipboard when our men . . . hoisted the Shallop out and took divers Turtles, there being an infinite number of them all over the sea as far as we could ken. By the end of the seventeenth century such observations were a thing of the past.","Mowat 1984, p. 188"</Row>
    <Row>96,"Champlain, ca 1610, noted that the sea-sturgeon were so abundant [in the territories of New France] that there might be sold in Germany, or other regions where this fish is much in demand, annually 100,000 livres worth.","Mowat 1984, p. 189"</Row>
    <Row>97,"Nicolas Denys gave sturgeon prominence in his listing of New World assets: There are some of eight, ten, eleven and twelve feed in length and as thich in the body as Sheepe ... That fish cometh to the entrance of rivers [and leaps] its own height above the water","Denys 1672, see Mowat 1984, p. 189"</Row>
    <Row>98,"New England 1614, Captain John Smith: [The Basse] are in such multitudes that I have seene stopped in the river ... at one tyde as many as will loade a ship of 100 tonnes ... at the turning of the tyde [I] have seen such multitudes pass out of a pounde [net] that it seemed to me one might go over their backs drishod.","Mowat 1984, p. 191"</Row>
    <Row>99,"During the peak years of the 1950s as many as 150,000 large bluefins [tuna] were killed annually in the North Atlantic commercial fishery -- but by 1973 the catch had fallen to 2,100 ... By 1960, more than 11,000 sports[fishers] were going out in charter boats every year ... One singularly enormous trap that has been operated in the Strait of Gibraltar since the sixth century caught 43,500 tuna as late as 1949; in 1982, its catch was 2,000 fish, all very small.","Mowat 1984, p. 193"</Row>
    <Row>100,"The Japanese made great strides in tuna long-lining, to the point where, by 1962, they were catching 400,000 tons of tuna a year. But by 1980 the entire Japanese fleet of 300 long-liners only managed to catch 4,000 tons of bluefin tuna.","Mowat 1984, p. 194"</Row>
    <Row>101,"In 1978, 3,000 giant [bluefin] tuna reached Japan, but by 1981 the Tuna Capital of the World was only able to catch fifty-five.","Mowat 1984, p. 195"</Row>
    <Row>102,"In colonial times, basking sharks were abundant in the Gulf of Maine, and thousands were killed off Cape Cod to produce liver oil for settlers' lamps","Mowat 1984, p. 197"</Row>
    <Row>103,"Fishermen found [basking sharks] a nuisance since they sometimes became fouled in nets. In consequence, the Canadian Department declared war on them in the 1940s. On the Pacific coast Fisheries protection vessels went after them first with harpoons. When these proved cumbersome and seldom fatal, rifle fire and even machine-gun fire wire tried .. One Fisheries patrol vessel cut down eighteen of the giants in one day.","Mowat 1984, p. 197"</Row>
    <Row>104,"In 1616, when Captain John Smith was extolling the virtues of New England -- You shall scarcely find any bay, shallow shore, or cove of sand where you may not take as many clams, or lobsters, or both as you please. -- he was in fact describing the entire Atlantic littoral from Cape Hateras to southern Labrador","Mowat 1984, p. 197"</Row>
    <Row>105,"Shellfish biologists, pondering the disappearance of oysters from so much of their aboriginal North American range, have tended to attribute this to climatic changes. They do not seem to realize that the easily harvested oysters were used as commercial fish bait ... In Cape Breton's Bras d'Or Lake as many as thirty schooners at a time once baited with the shellfish at St. Patrick's Channel. Oysters were but one source of shellfish bait. Clams and mussels served as well. Some nineteenth-century banking schooners loaded as much as ten tons of clams for a single voyage.","Mowat 1984, p. 198"</Row>
    <Row>106,"A solemn inland sea, studded with beautiful forest-clad islands, covered with towering trees whose branches at high tides are lapped by clear water teeming with fish","Captain George Vancouver describing the Strait of Georgia, 1792"</Row>
    <Row>107,"An ecosystem is a natural assemblage of organisms which, together with its habitat, has reached a survival level such that it is relatively independent of adjacent assemblages of equal rank; to this extent, given radiant energy, it is self-sustaining.

Allee, W.C., A.E. Emerson, O. Park, T. Park and K.P. Schmidt. 1949. Principles of Animal Ecology. W.B. Saunders, Philadelphia, 834 p.",Allee et al. 1949</Row>
    <Row>108,"An ecosystem is any entity or natural unit that includes living and nonliving parts interacting to produce a stable system in which the exchange of materials between the living and nonliving parts follows circular paths is an ecological system or ecosystem. The ecosystem is the largest functional unit in ecology, since it includes both organisms (biotic communities) and abiotic environment, each influencing the properties of the other and both necessary for maintenance of life as we have it on the earth. A lake is an example of an ecosystem.",Odum 1953</Row>
    <Row>109,"Sturgeon of immense size are plenty off the mouths of the Fraser and other rivers [in Strait of Georgia, B.C.] So abundant is this fish that isinglass made from it is a regular article of export by the Hudson's Bay Company","S. Wilkeson, 1817-1889"</Row>
    <Row>110,"The Indians catch [eulachon] by impaling them on rows of nails at the end of a stick about four feet long, and so thickly do they swarm, that every time this rude implement is waved in the water, two or three of them adhere to it",Macfie 1865</Row>
    <Row>111,"Mr Ashcroft, of Lytham, informs me that he was with the Red Cross fleet (Hull) on the Dogger Bank when the otter trawl was first tried there, on the steam trawler Madras, and that the difference between the catches of this vessel and the steam beam trawlers was so great that, as the boats went back to Hull for coal, they were not sent out again until they had the otter gear fitted, even if it took a week or ten days",Garstang 1900.</Row>
    <Row>112,"A maxim may, I think be laid down in the following formula, namely, that Nature ignores the existence of man. This is illustrated in the case of the herring by the fact that nature has placed in the sea such enemies of the herring as shall keep up the balance of power for the common good of all in the aquatic world, even if man were to fall out of existence altogether","Buckland 1878, p. 169"</Row>
    <Row>113,"To my mind the wondrous faculty that fish, especially herrings, and birds, have in shifting their quarters at regular well-marked seasons and to proper places is amply regulated by a faculty which does not exist at all in the cultivated human mind, and of which ordinary civilised people have no cognisance whatever. If, however, we are obliged to name in words the inducements which prompt migration amongst birds and fishes, the two words which would best express the idea would, as happily put by Gilbert White, be Love and Hunger","Buckland 1878, p 173"</Row>
    <Row>114,"During the autumnal herring season there are frequently from 8000 to 10,000 miles of nets in the North Sea at one time; and I am sure I am well within the mark when I say, that the herring-nets belonging to the fishermen of England and Scotland alone would, if placed end to end, considerably more than encircle the entire globe.","de Caux 1881, p. 119."</Row>
  </Table>
